 "notes": [
  "The tutorial has two diagrams (burn bootloader via ISP; upload via the Arduino's USB-serial with the microcontroller removed). This graph models the bootloader-burning circuit (first diagram), the full configuration with crystal, two 22 pF capacitors and 10k reset pull-up. The second diagram only differs by removing the Arduino's own chip and wiring Arduino TX/RX (pins 1/0) to the ATmega RX/TX (pins 2/3) instead of pins 10-13.",
  "The image labels the chip ATMEGA168 but the text uses ATmega328P; both are the same 28-pin DIP, modelled as atmega328p.",
  "ATmega328P pin mapping: 1 RESET, 7 VCC, 8 GND, 9 XTAL1, 10 XTAL2, 17 MOSI, 18 MISO, 19 SCK, 20 AVCC, 22 GND. Arduino pin 10 -> RESET, 11 -> MOSI, 12 -> MISO, 13 -> SCK per the standard ArduinoISP wiring.",
  "Exact breadboard columns/rows are my own placement: chip straddles the centre gap with pins 1-14 on the bottom half (row f, column n+2) and pins 15-28 on the top half (row e, column 31-n). Power rails are linked top to bottom by the red/black wires at the right edge, as in the image.",
  "The 10k resistor goes from RESET (pin 1) to 5V; the crystal sits across pins 9 and 10 and each pin has a 22 pF capacitor to GND. Pin 21 (AREF) is left unconnected.",
  "The first diagram shows four yellow wires; only three (pins 11, 12, 13) are needed plus the green wire from pin 10 for reset, which is how the text lists pins 10-13."
 ],
